So I started learning on my own on that Udemy course and then just building.

89
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:33,360
And it was a year later that I went to the boot camp and like actually learned a few like best practices and essentials and understood what things meant more directly.

90
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:36,320
But I wanted to build a meditation app for athletes.

91
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:37,600
I was a high school coach.

92
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:39,760
I talked about coaching golf and coaching basketball.

93
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,520
And I saw players struggling when the pressure was high.

94
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:49,200
I coached a ton of really great players, people playing Division 1 golf and basketball.

95
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:56,320
And it seemed like the best players had the most to lose and were most wrapped up in their identity around the sport.

96
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,000
And when it came time to get a college scholarship was like really the pivotal moment.

97
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:08,000
Many of them were miserable in their sport because of the expectations from themselves, from others.

98
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:22,000
And I saw an opportunity to help them with that moment with the pressure they were feeling and wanted to build an app to support them since most of the meditation content at that time felt a little too foo foo for like an athlete's identity.

99
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:24,880
And so I wanted to build something for them.

100
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:26,400
Yeah, that's an interesting one too.

101
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:40,240
I feel like a common thing I'm hearing always, but especially lately, is the like build for niches, like build the X for X, the Duolingo for middle aged moms or whatever.

102
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:44,640
And so that feels like it's almost exactly doing that kind of thing, right?

103
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:49,840
It's like building a meditation app, a very well-worn field, but for this one specific niche.

104
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:51,360
What was your approach to that?

105
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:54,240
Like what made it different than a normal meditation app then?

106
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:59,680
It was really the way it talked about the athletes experience.

107
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:07,520
I was so naive at the time that I thought I was like, oh, well, because I'd like have this X4Y, like sort of approach, like very niche.

108
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:12,240
mindset, I was going to build an app for golfers, an app for basketball players.

109
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,720
And then when I got to it, an app for athletes in general.

110
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,400
But I really cared about these two audiences.

111
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:18,720
I wanted them to have their own dedicated app.

112
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:21,040
And I thought I'd just like boom, boom, boom, roll it out.

113
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,600
It would all be out released within like six months of each other.

114
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:25,520
And that just didn't happen.

115
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:45,120
So like, basically, I could talk to the athletes in a way that they understood that put context around what they were facing in the sport and how they could use mental training and mindfulness, best practices and like bring it to the context that they were familiar with and talk to them in a way where they were used to being coached.

116
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,520
That was really the thing that I saw.

117
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:52,560
How am I going to get this education to the people who need it in a way that they're comfortable consuming it?

118
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:56,240
And then you got that to the store eventually, right?

119
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:56,960
Yeah.

120
00:08:57,280 --> 00:09:03,200
So I I started learning how to code in late 2015 within six to nine months.

121
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:07,280
I had an app on the app store and I was off and running and said, all right, here we go.

122
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:08,880
Now we're like raking the millions.

123
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:13,600
And did you rake in the millions and immediately find a massive audience that just came to you?

124
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:14,880
Oh, no.

125
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:17,200
If you build it, they will not come.

126
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:17,840
It turns out.

127
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,480
They will go do something more interesting with their lives.

128
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:23,120
And I just made every rookie mistake in the book.

129
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:25,680
And I'm so thankful I did now, ten years later.

130
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:33,520
But yeah, I mean, I subscribe to like the Eric Reese lean startup, like ship it early.

131
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:37,360
If you're not embarrassed of your first version, then you haven't shippedpped early enough.

132
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:40,240
And so I shipped it really early and it was really bare bones.

133
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:44,160
And I was super proud to have gone from knowing nothing to having it on the App Store.

134
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:47,200
But the market was uninterested.

135
00:09:47,600 --> 00:10:04,560
And as I went further and further and further, I learned that I really had made some pretty critical mistakes in the order that I did things and the way in which I understood what the people I was building for, like how they thought about the problem they were facing.

136
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:10,000
When you say, you made every rookie mistake in the book, what are those rookie mistakes?

137
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:11,840
Like, if you were to just kind of list them.

138
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:17,600
One of the big ones was, I guess, one assuming that if I just put it in the App Store, the downloads would start rolling in.

139
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:27,280
But another really big rookie mistake was I sent an email to every single college golf coach in the country, division one, division two, division three, men and women.

140
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:34,480
I just said, I emailed them all with like my best kind of cold sales email of like, hey, I have something that I think can help your audience.

141
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:36,160
Basically, nobody responded.

142
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,680
And a few responded unsubscribed.

143
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:41,520
One wrote me a really nasty email.

144
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:51,360
And then one coach said, hey, I'm kind of interested, but I would need you to have an Android app because I have a player on my team that has Android so I can't even look at this until you're on both platforms.

145
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:59,200
And so my rookie mistake was one person requested an Android app and I was like, well, oh, of course I need to be there too.

146
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:00,240
How can I make this happen?

147
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:05,760
And I basically turned around right away and started figuring out how can I be on Android?

148
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:13,600
And I rebuilt the app to be on Android so I can be on both platforms thinking that was the secret unlock to my success.

149
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:14,720
That's interesting.

150
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:18,640
So because it's like most of what you described, I don't think of as mistakes.

151
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:24,720
Like, yeah, sending a thousand emails and getting that in response, it's like, that is an unpleasant feeling, but it's.

152
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:26,080
a pleasant feeling, but it's, you know, shoot your shot.

153
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:27,360
Like, what's the harm?

154
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:32,640
But I guess the thing there is overweighting the first piece of feedback you got.

155
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:39,280
And in particular, in that case, it's the fact that it requires a pivot to fulfill that request, right?

156
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:43,760
It's one thing like responding really quickly to feedback, even if it feels niche.

157
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:50,000
If it's a small lift that can, that can be the thing that kind of generates momentum, I feel like for a lot of apps.

158
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:54,000
But creating an Android app is not a small, it's not a small lift.

159
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:56,640
And it's a thing you have to live with for the rest of it.

160
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:59,040
It kind of changes how fast you can move, right?

161
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:00,240
Uh, going forward.

162
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:00,640
Yeah.

163
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:03,680
Yeah, the mistake wasn't the emails, the stake, it wasn't the marketing.

164
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:24,560
It was, this was one of the key things, was like jumping straight to Android and thinking that just being on that second platform, having more options for people was the unlock, was the secret sauce to marketing, to showing up in a way that college teams and others would want to use this.

165
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:25,440
It just wasn't.

166
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:26,640
So that didn't take off.

167
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:29,040
Did you keep at it, keep building?

168
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:30,560
Obviously, you made an Android app.

169
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:34,640
Were you able to grow it into something or did it just kind of never get legs?

170
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:36,640
To cut to the point, it basically.

171
00:12:37,280 --> 00:13:05,520
never got legs but there were a few key things that happened with that shift to android so i started like looking for options for android i called around said hey who can build this for me and once again i still didn't have the money to pay somebody else to build an app for me so i was like okay and a student walked into my office and said hey mr duffett did you hear about this new thing called flutter i'm like no never heard of it search it up and say oh Okay, this is interesting.

172
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:10,000
Maybe this is what I'll do to be on both platforms.

173
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:11,520
And so I started learning Flutter.

174
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:18,960
And at that time, my I had made a phone call to a dev shop near me where I had a small relationship with the founder.

175
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:30,480
And he said, you know what, we can't help you, but there's this school down the street from us where every semester they have students and they need a client to work with for their final exam.

176
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:35,760
And they're going to do design work for you to help you refresh your screens and make your app look better.

177
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:40,240
And so I got to work with these three career changer, like UI UX.

178
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,840
students to take a brand new look at the app.

179
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:48,720
And something in that process like just opened my eyes.

180
00:13:49,280 --> 00:14:04,080
to like the difference that good design could make in just like the polish, the presentation and just thinking differently about how to convey what a user needs to like have access to and and how they move like through an app.

181
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:08,000
Working with those students was like I feel like such a pivotal moment.

182
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:18,720
And even though the app like never made more than a thousand or two thousand dollars in a single year and ironically like had its best years ever the year I didn't pay attention to it at all.

183
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:22,000
It seems to be how it was COVID.

184
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:23,680
So I think that was the key piece.

185
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:24,400
Yeah.

186
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:35,280
That was such a huge moment in, like, my, like, pay it, pay it forward to my future self, like, go through the, the hard learning experiences to now.

187
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:43,840
I feel like the design, the presentation of what I'm building now, the shop pattern is part of what separates it and why it found a market at all.

188
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:48,800
What was it about that process that helped you gain those insights?

189
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:54,880
Was it, were you working directly with them and sort of like trying to explain yourself to them and that's the process?

190
00:14:55,120 --> 00:15:00,880
Or were they bringing in like lessons that they learned and and techniques and everything that you sort of learned to then apply?

191
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:39,120
i think it was more of the techniques because they there's three of them they each had to come with their own ideas for like what the best look would be i don't know if they kind of got pigeonholed into a certain aesthetic like one was very gradient focused and i don't know if he was like forced to be working with oh yeah like they just were assigned a certain technique or something yeah so i never asked and they never said but i started to get that feel but the way they worked through it of like they they showed me three dozen apps and said which like of these which of these are ones that you like and which are ones that you don't like.

192
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:47,440
And then they, like every few weeks, they would come to me like, here's our latest approach to like what we think you should do with the app.

193
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:50,960
And there was just these like really nice.

194
00:15:51,600 --> 00:16:02,800
touches of like how they would layer text on top of images and the way they would use like shading and blurs and gradients so that the text was readable.

195
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:09,360
There's enough contrast, but yet it had all of this beautiful imagery and the way to fit those two together.

196
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:24,880
I found myself like using those concepts over and over again when I build a presentation for class or when I put together a little slideshow or when I make a screen of just like you can use basically these layers in between the text and the images so that it all works well together.

197
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:25,760
That's interesting.

198
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:36,080
It's almost like, you know, working with coworkers where simply through that process of being around and seeing other people's work, you're bringing in different ideas and everything.

199
